---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

const description =
  'Import your history from FitNotes, Strong, Hevy, or StrongLifts, then pick the guide that fits how you train.';

// One entry per app someone arrives from. FitNotes, Strong and Hevy each have
// a page, because each import genuinely differs: a FitNotes backup is a
// database and carries the library and routines, Strong's CSV carries RPE and
// per-set notes, Hevy's marks set type so warmups survive.
// Re-checked 2026-10-23 for 1.1.15: FitNotes routines now import; Strong's
// Set Order W/F/D markers and Workout Notes, Hevy's description and supersets,
// and StrongLifts body weight all come across.
//
// StrongLifts is last and points at the 5x5 program page on purpose. That page
// already carries the import walkthrough, and a second page of ours aimed at
// the same query would split it rather than adding anything.
const fromApps = [
  {
    app: 'FitNotes',
    href: '/guides/fitnotes-alternative/',
    note: 'The backup is a database, so your categories, library, routines, body weight and measurements come too.',
    full: true,
  },
  {
    app: 'Strong',
    href: '/guides/strong-alternative/',
    note: 'A CSV that brings your RPE and your notes with it, and warmups and failure sets when the export marks them.',
    full: true,
  },
  {
    app: 'Hevy',
    href: '/guides/hevy-alternative/',
    note: 'The richest CSV of the four. It marks set type, so your warmups arrive as warmups.',
    full: true,
  },
  {
    app: 'StrongLifts',
    href: '/guides/stronglifts-5x5-tracker/#import',
    note: 'Covered on the 5x5 page, together with the template that ships in the app.',
    full: false,
  },
];
---

<HelpLayout title="Switching from another workout tracker" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Switching to Badger from another workout tracker"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-24"
    about={['FitNotes', 'Strong', 'Hevy', 'StrongLifts']}
    breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' }]}
  />

  <h1>Switching to Badger</h1>
  <p>Coming from another tracker? Bring your history over first so your PRs and graphs start out full, then pick the guide that fits how you train.</p>

  <h2>Your first 5 minutes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Save an export from your old app. <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">Importing from another app</a> says where each app keeps it.</li>
    <li>In Badger, open the import page during first-launch onboarding, or later go to <strong>Settings → Your data</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose your old app: FitNotes, Strong, Hevy, or StrongLifts. In onboarding, the main button then reads <strong>Import from</strong> that app. The screen shows that app's export steps first; tap <strong>Choose file</strong> and pick the file.</li>
    <li>Check the preview: exercises, workout days, sets, and the weight unit. Hevy and StrongLifts files set the unit. Strong and FitNotes show a <strong>Weight unit in this file</strong> choice to check.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Import</strong>. Badger saves a safety copy of your current log first, so you can go back.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What carries over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your exercises, your workouts, and every set in them.</li>
    <li>Weights in their original unit, with no rounding or conversion.</li>
    <li>New exercises land in an "Imported" category for you to sort. A FitNotes backup is the exception: it brings your own categories.</li>
    <li>Sets on a date where you already have a session are added to it. Nothing you logged is overwritten.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The 3 things to know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your PRs are worked out from the imported history, with no extra step.</li>
    <li>Import each file once. A second import adds its sets again on any day you already have, and the preview warns you before that happens.</li>
    <li>Only a FitNotes backup brings routines. From any other app, start from a <a href="/help/routines/#programs">built-in program</a> or build your own.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="your-old-app">Your old app</h2>
  <p>How much comes across depends on what your old app exports.</p>

  <ul class="from-rows">
    {fromApps.map((a) => (
      <li>
        <a class="from-row" href={a.href}>
          <span class="from-row__app">{a.app}</span>
          <span class="from-row__note">{a.note}</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Now pick your path</h2>
  <p>With your history in place, choose the guide that fits how you train: <a href="/guides/just-lifting/">just lifting</a>, <a href="/guides/program-runner/">running a program</a>, or <a href="/guides/optimizer/">tracking the data</a>. Or browse <a href="/guides/overview/">everything Badger does</a>.</p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* Same ledger row as the guide picker on /guides: label left, note under or
     beside it. Reused rather than reinvented so the two indexes read as one
     site. */
  .from-rows {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 26px;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .from-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 16px;
    padding: 13px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .from-row:hover {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .from-row__app {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    min-width: 110px;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .from-row:hover .from-row__app {
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .from-row__note {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .from-row {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 2px;
    }

    .from-row__app {
      min-width: 0;
    }
  }
</style>
